      </figure>
      <div class="note"><b>Scripture:</b> "In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth." (<i>Genesis 1:1</i>) — God as Creator. On the incarnation: "The Word became flesh and dwelt among us." (<i>John 1:14</i>)</div>
    </section>

    <!-- 7 MISCONCEPTION: Trinity -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="trinity-misconception">
      <span class="tag lo">Watch out</span>
      <h2>The Trinity is not three gods</h2>
      <p>A very common exam error is to say the Trinity means Christians believe in three gods. This is <b>wrong</b>. The Trinity is the teaching that the <b>one</b> God exists as three <b>persons</b> — Father, Son and Holy Spirit — who are distinct but share one divine nature.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Say it like this:</b> "Christianity is monotheistic. The Trinity means one God in three persons — not three separate gods." That single sentence protects you from the most common mistake in this part of the topic.</p>
    </section>

