    <!-- 11 JUDAISM -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="judaism">
      <span class="tag lo">Family, sex &amp; contraception</span>
      <h2>Family life and relationships</h2>
      <p>Christians value the <b>family</b> as where children are loved, cared for and taught the faith. Many hold that sex belongs within marriage; some Christians accept <b>cohabitation</b> and sex before marriage, while more traditional churches do not. Views on the <b>roles of men and women</b> vary — some emphasise equal partnership, others distinct roles.</p>
      <div class="note"><b>Scripture:</b> "Children, obey your parents… Fathers, do not exasperate your children." (<i>Ephesians 6:1–4</i>) — mutual duty within the family.</div>
      <p>On <b>contraception</b> and family planning Christians differ: the <b>Catholic Church</b> teaches that artificial contraception is wrong (favouring natural methods), while many <b>Protestant</b> churches accept contraception as a responsible choice within marriage.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 12 HINDUISM -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="hinduism">
      <span class="tag lo">Matters of life and death</span>
      <h2>Sanctity of life &amp; abortion</h2>
      <p>Christians believe in the <span class="hl t">sanctity of life</span>: life is <b>holy and God-given</b>, so it should be protected. This belief, with the command "You shall not murder" (Exodus 20:13), shapes Christian views on <b>abortion</b>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The <b>Catholic Church</b> and many conservative Christians teach that life begins at conception, so abortion is normally <b>wrong</b>.</li>
        <li>Some Christians accept abortion in limited cases — for example to save the mother's life, or after rape — showing compassion (a <b>quality of life</b> concern).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Christians who differ still take the sanctity of life seriously; the debate is about how to apply it with love and mercy.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Careful:</b> avoid saying "all Christians think abortion is always wrong" — mainstream teaching leans that way, but there is real variety in how Christians weigh compassion.</p>
    </section>

