    <!-- 14 BUDDHISM -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="buddhism">
      <span class="tag lo">Matters of life and death</span>
      <h2>Euthanasia &amp; life after death</h2>
      <p>Because life is God-given, most Christians oppose <span class="hl el">euthanasia</span> (deliberately ending life to relieve suffering): they teach that only God should decide when life ends, and they support <b>hospice care</b> instead. Some Christians, out of compassion, feel a case can be made in extreme suffering — but this is a minority view.</p>
      <p>Christians believe in <b>life after death</b>. Because Jesus rose from the dead (the <b>resurrection</b>), Christians hope for eternal life, and the belief in <b>judgement</b>, heaven and hell gives meaning to how they live now.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Scripture:</b> "I am the resurrection and the life. Whoever believes in me will live, even though they die." (<i>John 11:25</i>) — Jesus on life after death.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 15 SIKHISM -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="sikhism">
      <span class="tag lo">Creation, evolution &amp; the Big Bang</span>
      <h2>Science and creation</h2>
      <p>Christians believe God is the <span class="hl c">Creator</span> (Genesis 1). They respond to <b>evolution</b> and the <b>Big Bang</b> in different ways:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Many Christians accept science, seeing the Big Bang and evolution as <b>how God created</b> — reading Genesis as poetic truth, not a science textbook.</li>
        <li>Some Christians (often called <b>Creationists</b>) read Genesis more literally and reject evolution.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note"><b>Scripture:</b> "In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth." (<i>Genesis 1:1</i>) — the key claim is <b>that</b> God created, whatever the process.</div>
      <p>For most Christians there is <b>no war</b> between faith and science: science describes the "how", while faith addresses <b>who</b> and <b>why</b>.</p>
    </section>

