    <!-- 19 TRANSCENDENT vs IMMANENT -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="transcendent-immanent">
      <span class="tag lo">Christian ethical reasoning</span>
      <h2>How Christians decide right and wrong</h2>
      <p>Christians use several tools to make moral decisions:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="hl t">Sanctity of life</span> — because life is God-given, protect it (shapes abortion and euthanasia views).</li>
        <li><span class="hl el">Agape love</span> — the selfless love Jesus taught; "Love your neighbour as yourself" (Mark 12:31).</li>
        <li><span class="hl g">Situation ethics</span> — the view that the loving thing (agape) should decide each case, rather than fixed rules.</li>
        <li><span class="hl k">Teachings of Jesus</span> — his example and words (e.g. the Sermon on the Mount) guide Christian conduct.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Christians weigh these differently: some follow firm rules, others (like situation ethics) put love first — so on hard issues Christians can reach different conclusions.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Scripture:</b> "Love is patient, love is kind… it always protects, always trusts, always hopes." (<i>1 Corinthians 13</i>) — Paul's description of agape.</p>
    </section>

